PHASE 7 — ADMIN DASHBOARD + PRODUCT MANAGEMENT

## Context

This is Phase 7 of the Bantex Trading website rebuild.

All phases are stored inside the `phases` folder.

You must work ONLY on Phase 7.

Do not start Phase 8 or implement features belonging to later phases unless they are strictly required for the admin foundation.

The Bantex Trading frontend design is already approved. Do NOT redesign the public website.

The admin dashboard should have its own clean, professional interface while still feeling like the same Bantex Trading brand.

---

# Goal

Build a real admin dashboard that allows the owner to manage the products and catalogue shown on the public website.

The owner should be able to:

* Add products
* Edit products
* Delete products
* Add/remove categories
* Manage prices
* Manage product images
* Manage product options/variants
* Control product availability
* Manage which products appear in each category

The public website should use the managed product data rather than hard-coded product information.

---

# 1. Admin Route

Create a dedicated admin area, for example:

`/admin`

Build a proper dashboard layout with:

* Sidebar/navigation
* Dashboard header
* Main content area
* Responsive mobile navigation

Possible navigation:

* Overview
* Products
* Categories
* Orders
* Settings

However, ONLY implement the sections required by this phase.

Orders can remain a placeholder/navigation item for a future phase.

Do not build unnecessary dashboard features.

---

# 2. Admin Authentication

The admin area must not be publicly accessible.

Implement a secure authentication approach appropriate for the project's backend architecture.

Do NOT create a fake frontend-only password check such as:

`if (password === "admin123")`

Do not hard-code admin credentials.

Use secure authentication and environment/database-backed credentials where appropriate.

Protect all admin routes server-side.

An unauthenticated user attempting to access `/admin` should be redirected to the appropriate login page.

---

# 3. Product Management

Create a Products section.

The owner must be able to:

* View products
* Search products
* Add a product
* Edit a product
* Delete a product
* Enable/disable availability
* Assign a category
* Set a price
* Upload/manage images

Each product should have a proper editable form.

---

# 4. Product Fields

The product model should support at least:

* Product ID
* Name
* Slug
* Description
* Price
* Images
* Category
* Availability
* Options/variants
* Created date
* Updated date

Do not create unnecessary fields just for the sake of having them.

Keep the data model extensible.

---

# 5. Product Options / Variants

This is important.

The admin must NOT assume every product has the same options.

Allow products to have different option types such as:

* Colour
* Size
* Model
* Capacity
* Pack size
* Specification
* Custom option

The owner should be able to add an option group and define its values.

Example:

Option:

`Colour`

Values:

`Black`
`Blue`
`Red`

Another product could have:

`Size`

Values:

`Small`
`Medium`
`Large`

Another could have:

`Model`

Values:

`Model A`
`Model B`

The system must support different combinations for different products.

---

# 6. Variant Pricing

Design the data structure so variants can support their own pricing when necessary.

For example:

Base product:

`Product A`

Variant:

`Large + Black`

could have a different price from:

`Small + Black`

Do not force every product to have variant-specific pricing if it does not need it.

The architecture should support:

* One base price
* Optional variant-specific price

The authoritative price must ultimately come from the server-side product data.

---

# 7. Product Images

Allow the admin to manage product images.

Support:

* Main product image
* Additional product images
* Image ordering
* Removing images
* Replacing images

The public product detail page should use these images automatically.

Do not hard-code image URLs into individual product pages.

Use the existing image architecture or create a clean abstraction that can later connect to proper storage.

---

# 8. Categories

Create a Categories management section.

The owner must be able to:

* Create a category
* Rename a category
* Delete a category
* Enable/disable a category
* Assign products to categories

Do not hard-code the category system so that only Stationery and Electrical Goods can ever exist.

The architecture should allow additional categories later.

However, preserve the existing public Bantex categories and routes.

Do not break:

* `/stationery`
* `/electrical`

If the public category structure needs mapping between editable categories and routes, create that mapping cleanly.

---

# 9. Public Website Integration

This is one of the most important parts of Phase 7.

Products created or edited in the admin dashboard must appear correctly on the public website.

Changes should affect:

* Stationery catalogue
* Electrical Goods catalogue
* Product cards
* Product detail pages
* Prices
* Images
* Availability
* Options/variants

Do not maintain separate duplicated product definitions for admin and frontend.

Use one authoritative product data source.

---

# 10. Database / Persistence

Product and category data must persist.

Do not build an admin dashboard where changes disappear after a page refresh.

Use the project's appropriate persistent database/storage architecture.

If a database has already been established in the project, use it rather than introducing a second unnecessary database.

Create proper:

* Product records
* Category records
* Option records
* Variant records where required
* Image references

Use relationships rather than duplicating large amounts of product data.

---

# 11. Validation

Admin forms must validate data.

Examples:

* Product name required
* Price must be valid
* Category must be valid
* Required option values must be valid
* Slugs must be valid
* Invalid variant combinations must be rejected
* Duplicate category names should be handled appropriately

Show useful validation messages.

Do not allow malformed product data to break the public website.

---

# 12. Delete Protection

Be careful with deletion.

If deleting a category that still contains products, do not silently orphan those products.

Provide an appropriate handling flow, such as:

* Prevent deletion until products are reassigned
* Or require reassignment before deletion

For products, use appropriate confirmation before permanent deletion.

Do not make destructive actions one-click with no protection.

---

# 13. Availability

The admin should be able to mark a product as:

* Available
* Unavailable

The public website should reflect this state.

An unavailable product should not incorrectly appear as purchasable.

Do not implement full inventory/stock management in this phase.

Availability is enough for now.

---

# 14. Product Ordering

Allow the admin to control the display order of products where practical.

This should allow the owner to determine which products appear first.

Keep this simple.

Do not build an unnecessarily complex drag-and-drop CMS unless it genuinely improves the implementation.

---

# 15. Dashboard Overview

Create a simple admin overview page showing useful information such as:

* Total products
* Available products
* Total categories
* Recently updated products

Do not fabricate statistics.

Use actual database values.

Do not build analytics, revenue charts, or fake metrics.

---

# 16. Security

Admin operations must be protected server-side.

Verify authorization for:

* Creating products
* Editing products
* Deleting products
* Creating categories
* Editing categories
* Deleting categories
* Uploading/removing images

Do not rely only on hiding buttons from unauthorized users.

Never expose database credentials or private API keys to the browser.

Validate all mutations on the server.

---

# 17. Stripe Compatibility

Do not break Phase 6.

The existing checkout/payment architecture must continue working.

Product prices used by checkout must come from the same authoritative product data managed by the admin system.

Do not allow the admin UI to bypass server-side validation.

Do not modify Stripe payment logic unless necessary to connect it to the new product data source.

---

# 18. Existing Cart Compatibility

Do not break Phase 5.

Existing cart items should continue to work with:

* Product IDs
* Selected options
* Quantities
* Product prices

If the product structure changes, maintain compatibility where reasonably possible.

Remember that the checkout phase must revalidate the authoritative product and price server-side.

---

# 19. UI Requirements

The admin dashboard should be:

* Clean
* Professional
* Fast
* Easy to understand
* Responsive
* Suitable for frequent use

Use the Bantex visual language without copying the public homepage unnecessarily.

Avoid:

* Excessive gradients
* Excessive shadows
* Generic dashboard templates
* Fake analytics
* Decorative charts
* Unnecessary animations
* Excessive icons

Prioritize usability.

---

# 20. Empty States

Handle empty states properly.

Examples:

No products:

`No products have been added yet.`

No categories:

`No categories have been created yet.`

Do not fill the admin dashboard with fake products or fake statistics.

---

# 21. Error Handling

Handle:

* Database failures
* Unauthorized access
* Invalid product data
* Invalid category data
* Image upload failures
* Duplicate slugs
* Failed saves
* Failed deletes

Give the admin a useful error message without exposing internal secrets or stack traces.

